  "result": "Not quite. A model doesn't remember anything between conversations on its own. Each call starts blank, and it only knows what's in the text you send it that time.\n\nHere's what that looks like for your assistant:\n\n- **Last week:** a student says \"I'm certified on the laser cutter, but not the CNC.\" The assistant replies, and then the model forgets all of it.\n- **This week:** the student asks \"Can I book the CNC?\" The model has no idea who they are or what they said.\n\nTo fix it, your program has to do the remembering. It saves \"laser cutter: certified, CNC: not yet\" somewhere, like a file or database. Next session it loads that note and puts it into the text it sends the model. Then the model \"remembers\", because the note is right there in front of it.\n\nThis matters for your design because memory is something you build. It's also a cost, since every saved note you re-send uses up your budget.\n\nIf you were saving just one line per student, what would it be?",
